text: >-
  provided approximately 100% of discoverable material in its possession to the
  defense. In other

  words, the defense has what the State has. And much of this discovery was
  presented long ago.

  On December 11, 2025, the preliminary hearing was set for the week of May 18,
  2026. On

  March 12, 2026, the State informed defense counsel of its witness and
  exhibits. Concurrent with

  this filing, the State provided defense counsel a witness list containing the
  names of its anticipated

  witnesses, a list of approximately thiry-five exhibits it intends to offer,
  together with Bates

  numbers for the exhibits and Bates numbers for reports describing the
  testimony of the witnesses.

  The list also contains the dates on which each item of discovery was provided
  to the defense.

  With the exception of a photograph of a burnt note (provided on January 9,
  2026), doorbell

  surveillance from a single property (provided January 21, 2026), a DNA report
  regarding items

  found in Defendant's apartment (provided January 21, 2026), and a toolmarks
  report (provided

  March 12, 2026), each of the witnesses and exhibits have been known to the
  defense for at least

  six months before the preliminary hearing.' Most of the evidence to be
  presented at the preliminary

  hearing, including the DNA report regarding the rifle found at the scene, was
  provided to the

  defense in October of 2025. The State does not intend to offer evidence
  obtained through forensic

  extractions of electronics seized in this case. It does, however, intend to
  offer two exhibits which

  used cameras to record the contents of text messages. These recordings were
  provided to the

  defense on October 24, 2025.

  ARGUMENT

  1 The State is currently in the process of obtaining a small number 1102
  statements from witnesses whose

  involvement was described in police reports.

  2
